WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
<c>Welcome to this practical frame weaving demonstration</c>

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Today we inspect a small repeatable video workflow

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
First the source metadata identifies the recording safely

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Then captions provide a fast local transcript path

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Manual tracks preserve the speaker's intended wording

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Automatic tracks often repeat their recent caption lines

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The parser removes that overlap without losing speech

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Clean timestamps keep every observation in proper order.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Short cues accumulate and I think so

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
so This opening section ends with a clear sentence

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Next we compare the visible frames with spoken context

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Each sampled image receives a stable identifier

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Primary frames begin where a speech segment begins

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Extra frames cover meaningful changes inside longer passages

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
The frame file name also carries its timestamp

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Agents can inspect the image when exact text matters

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Descriptions summarize the screen without replacing ground truth

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Quoted strings record only what the model can read

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Uncertain lettering stays explicitly marked as illegible

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
This second section closes at another sentence.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Now the transcript joins evidence from both channels

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Speech remains verbatim from the selected caption track

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Visual summaries remain clearly labeled as model descriptions

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Relative paths make the complete folder easy to move

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Small JSON sidecars keep structured provenance available

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Plain text keeps the main artifact easy to read

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Stable segment identifiers support later frame associations

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Sentence boundaries produce coherent chunks for downstream agents

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The fallback boundary stays close to thirty seconds

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
This third section completes the provenance explanation.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Finally the run reports every resource it consumed

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Caption fetching uses no vision quota or dollars

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Rate limits become warnings instead of fatal errors

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Missing tracks fall through to local speech recognition

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Client audio never leaves the machine for transcription

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Temporary files become visible only after atomic publication

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Tests replay fixed fixtures without touching the network

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Sequential ids make ordering obvious to every reader

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
The final artifact preserves its raw English&nbsp;caption track

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The demonstration finishes with one last complete sentence!
